In the Web Audio API, audio units can act as inputs to other audio units. This allows for techniques such as ring modulation at very low computational cost.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
    <head>
        <script>
            var ctx = undefined;
            function toggle() {
                if (ctx) {
                    ctx.close();
                    ctx = undefined;
                } else {
                    ctx = new AudioContext();
                    var osc = ctx.createOscillator();
                    osc.frequency.value = 880.0;
                    var gain = ctx.createGain();
                    gain.gain.value = 0.05;
                    var mod = ctx.createOscillator();
                    mod.frequency.value = 200.0;
          var rmod = ctx.createGain();
          mod.connect(rmod.gain);
          rmod.connect(gain);
                    osc.connect(rmod);
                    gain.connect(ctx.destination);
                    osc.start();
          mod.start();
                }
            }
        </script>
    </head>
    <body>
        <button onclick="toggle()">Toggle audio</button>
    </body>
</html>
```

This PR brings this technique to wags. It is exploratory in nature: the first-pass attempt is the naïve (and potentially sufficient) one of adding `Node` to `AudioParameter`. If that doesn't work, we can zoom out and try to assess what's wrong.

